          },
          {
            "bioctype": "BioCPassage",
            "offset": 4145,
            "infons": {
              "section_type": "DISCUSS",
              "type": "title_1"
            },
            "text": "DISCUSSION",
            "sentences": [],
            "annotations": [],
            "relations": []
          },
          {
            "bioctype": "BioCPassage",
            "offset": 4156,
            "infons": {
              "section_type": "DISCUSS",
              "type": "paragraph"
            },
            "text": "The corridor’s validity is a principal strength, particularly at the L4–L5 and L5–S1 levels [Figure 2]. It preserves the multifidus muscle to maintain segmental spinal stability and prevent the atrophy typically associated with open midline subperiosteal dissection. MIS adaptations, including hydrodissection and tubular retractors, have further expanded its utility for MIS-TLIF (Wiltse and Spencer 1988) [Figure 3]. Primary perioperative advantages include reduced intraoperative blood loss, lower pain scores, and faster recovery. Crucially, these early benefits do not compromise long-term efficacy, as fusion rates and functional outcomes remain comparable for both procedures. Despite biological advantages, underutilization persists in some centers, requiring standardized anatomical education and technical guidelines [Table 1].",
            "sentences": [],
            "annotations": [],
            "relations": []
          },
          {
            "bioctype": "BioCPassage",
            "offset": 5000,
            "infons": {
              "section_type": "CONCL",
              "type": "title_1"
            },
            "text": "CONCLUSION",
            "sentences": [],
            "annotations": [],
            "relations": []
          },
          {
            "bioctype": "BioCPassage",
            "offset": 5011,
            "infons": {
              "section_type": "CONCL",
              "type": "paragraph"
            },
            "text": "The Wiltse paraspinal approach represents a biologically sound, anatomically validated corridor for lumbar surgery. Its major advantages include reduced blood loss and pain while maintaining long-term clinical outcomes comparable to traditional open midline techniques.",
            "sentences": [],
            "annotations": [],
            "relations": []
          },
          {
